What each licence asks of us
----------------------------

MIT, ISC, BSD-2-Clause
  Permissive. Keep the copyright notice and licence text with any distribution
  that includes the code. No obligation to publish our own source.

Apache-2.0 (idb-keyval)
  Permissive, plus an explicit patent grant. If a package ships a NOTICE file,
  that notice must be carried through in redistributions. Its terms are
  permissive, so incorporating Apache-2.0 code into LUME is unproblematic.

Unlicense (tweetnacl, tweetnacl-util)
  Public-domain dedication. Nothing is required of us. Worth stating plainly
  because these two carry the cryptographic primitives: no licence obstacle,
  but also no warranty and no upstream obligation to anyone.

MPL-2.0 (web-push)
  The only dependency with copyleft attached. It is file-level: if we modify a
  source file that came from web-push, that file stays MPL-2.0 and its source
  must be made available. Merely depending on it — which is what we do — carries
  no such duty, so combining it into LUME is fine.

  If web-push ever gets patched in place rather than upgraded, that patch is the
  case this paragraph exists for.


Assets and branding
-------------------

Material under `brand/` is project-owned and is not covered by the dependency
licences above.

LUME is not affiliated with Telegram, Signal, or any other messenger. Where the
repository documents ideas studied in other projects, those are design
conclusions written from scratch — no third-party source was copied, and no
third-party trademark or asset is used.


Cryptography and export
-----------------------

LUME performs end-to-end encryption on the client using X25519, Ed25519,
HKDF-SHA256 and XSalsa20-Poly1305, via `tweetnacl` and `@noble/hashes`. It
implements no cryptography of its own design.

Distributing cryptographic software is regulated in some jurisdictions. This
file does not assess those obligations; anyone redistributing LUME should check
what applies where they operate.


Keeping this file honest
------------------------

The lists above were generated from the `dependencies` fields of
`client/package.json` and `server/package.json` and the `license` field of each
installed package. They cover direct runtime dependencies only — not transitive
ones, and not `devDependencies`.

Regenerate after any dependency change:

    node -e "const fs=require('fs'),p=require('path');for(const d of ['client','server']){const j=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(d+'/package.json','utf8'));console.log('== '+d);for(const n of Object.keys(j.dependencies||{})){let l='?';for(const b of [d+'/node_modules/','node_modules/']){try{l=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(p.join(b,n,'package.json'),'utf8')).license||'?';break}catch(e){}}console.log(n+'  '+l)}}"

A dependency that appears in the output but not above means this file is stale.
